cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Historical sales opportunity performance analysis\r

      \r

      #### **Project overview**\r

      <small>\r

      You are a business analyst supporting the sales leadership team of a
      fictional B2B technology company. Leadership wants to better understand
      historical sales performance across completed opportunities.\r

      \r

      Your task is to use the company’s Oracle database to investigate where
      sales outcomes differ across customers, opportunity types, sales
      organization, and products. You will communicate findings and recommend
      actions that sales leadership should investigate, monitor, or improve.\r

      \r

      This project uses descriptive and diagnostic analytics only. You will not
      build predictive models, use logistic regression, score opportunities, or
      recommend which individual live opportunities to prioritize.\r

      \r

      <b>Business scenario</b><br>\r

      Sales leadership has many completed sales opportunities in its CRM system.
      Management wants to understand:\r

      - Where did historical sales performance differ, and what should sales
      leadership investigate, monitor, or improve?\r

      \r

      Your analysis should help leadership understand historical patterns in win
      rates, completed sales-cycle duration, product mix, and sales activity.
      Your conclusions must be supported by evidence from the database.\r

      \r

      A finding of “no meaningful difference” is a valid result when supported
      by your analysis.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **S - Specify the question**\r

      <small>\r

      <b>Primary business question</b><br>\r

      Among completed opportunities, where did historical win rate, sales-cycle
      duration, and product mix differ, and what should sales leadership
      investigate, monitor, or improve?\r

      \r

      \r

      You must also develop two supporting analytical questions.\r

      \r

      Examples include:\r

      - How do win rates differ by customer industry, customer geography, or
      account revenue band?\r

      - How do outcomes differ by opportunity type, business unit, sales team,
      or owner?\r

      - How does completed sales-cycle duration differ between won and lost
      opportunities?\r

      - How do push counts differ between won and lost opportunities?\r

      - Which products appear most often in won and lost opportunities?\r

      - Do opportunities containing multiple products differ from single-product
      opportunities?\r

      \r

      You may develop other supporting questions if they are answerable with the
      available data. Your analysis may identify associations or differences
      between groups. Do not claim that a factor caused an outcome unless the
      data and method support a causal conclusion.\r

      <small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Analytical question #1</b><br>\r

      <b>Question:</b> How does historical win rate differ by customer
      industry?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Win rate (won opportunities ÷ total
      completed opportunities), grouped by customer industry, with the
      underlying opportunity count reported alongside each rate.<br>\r

      <b>Relevance:</b> Identifying industries with higher or lower historical
      win rates helps leadership understand where the company closes deals more
      effectively and where sales messaging, qualification, or resourcing may
      need attention.<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name:</b> Win Rate by Industry\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "/*\r

      Analytical question # 1 SQL\r

      This should be a SQL query that returns the data\r

      necessary to answer the analytical question.\r

      You'll need to create a visualization based on the query results\r

      that effectively communicates the insights derived from the data.\r

      */\r

      -- Place your query below this line.\r

      \r

      SELECT \r

      \    a.INDUSTRY,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      won_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Lost' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      lost_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        SUM(CASE WHEN o.STAGE = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) * 100.0 /
      COUNT(*),\r

      \        1\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT a\r

      \    ON o.ACCOUNT_ID = a.ACCOUNT_ID\r

      GROUP BY a.INDUSTRY\r

      ORDER BY win_rate_pct DESC;\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Analytical question #2</b><br>\r

      <b>Question:</b> Do multi-product opportunities differ from single-product
      opportunities in historical win rate and completed sales-cycle
      duration<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Completed opportunity count, historical
      win rate, and median completed sales-cycle duration for single-product
      versus multi-product<br>\r

      <b>Relevance:</b> This analysis evaluates whether there are differences
      between single and multi product opportunities, which would answer the
      ultimate business question of: \"how much benefit is there from
      product-mix\"<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name:</b> Single vs Multi-product Outcomes\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "/*\r

      Analytical question # 2 SQL\r

      This should be a SQL query that returns the data\r

      necessary to answer the analytical question.\r

      You'll need to create a visualization based on the query results\r

      that effectively communicates the insights derived from the data.\r

      */\r

      -- Place your query below this line.\r

      WITH product_counts AS (\r

      \    SELECT\r

      \        opportunity_id,\r

      \        COUNT(DISTINCT product_id) AS distinct_product_count\r

      \    FROM database_fundamentals.opportunity_line_item\r

      \    GROUP BY opportunity_id\r

      )\r

      \r

      SELECT\r

      \    CASE\r

      \        WHEN pc.distinct_product_count = 1 THEN 'Single-product'\r

      \        ELSE 'Multi-product'\r

      \    END AS product_group,\r

      \r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_opportunities,\r

      \r

      \    SUM(\r

      \        CASE\r

      \            WHEN o.stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1\r

      \            ELSE 0\r

      \        END\r

      \    ) AS won_opportunities,\r

      \r

      \    SUM(\r

      \        CASE\r

      \            WHEN o.stage = 'Closed Lost' THEN 1\r

      \            ELSE 0\r

      \        END\r

      \    ) AS lost_opportunities,\r

      \r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        100.0 *\r

      \        SUM(CASE WHEN o.stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END)\r

      \        / COUNT(*),\r

      \        2\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct,\r

      \r

      \    MEDIAN(o.age) AS median_cycle_days\r

      \r

      FROM database_fundamentals.opportunity o\r

      JOIN product_counts pc\r

      \    ON o.opportunity_id = pc.opportunity_id\r

      \r

      WHERE o.stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      \r

      GROUP BY\r

      \    CASE\r

      \        WHEN pc.distinct_product_count = 1 THEN 'Single-product'\r

      \        ELSE 'Multi-product'\r

      \    END\r

      \r

      ORDER BY product_group;\r\n"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Anticipated data limitations relevant to your analytical
      questions</b><br>\r

      Since the analysis is limited to historical opportunities, differences
      between industries or between single- and multi-product opportunities can
      only be interpreted as associations rather than causation. Industry-level
      comparisons, because of their large scope, may mitigate the effects of
      individual accounts, regions, or types of opportunities. Similarly,
      organizing opportunities as either single- or multi-product opportunities
      does not capture the nuances of product combinations or specific products.
      The sizes of the groups between single- and multi-product opportunities
      were also different, becoming another obstacle to making a precise
      comparison.\r

      <small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **O - Obtain the data**\r

      \r

      <small>\r

      <b>Data source</b><br>\r

      You will work with the fictional Salesforce-style data in the
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS Oracle schema.<br>\r

      \r

      <b>Relevant tables may include:</b>\r

      \r

      <table>\r

      \  <thead>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <th>Table</th>\r

      \      <th>General content</th>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </thead>\r

      \  <tbody>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OPPORTUNITY</td>\r

      \      <td>Historical sales opportunities and final outcomes</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM</td>\r

      \      <td>Products and quantities associated with opportunities</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>ACCOUNT</td>\r

      \      <td>Customer/account characteristics</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>OWNER</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales-owner characteristics</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>PRODUCT</td>\r

      \      <td>Product names and catalog prices</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>BUSINESS_UNIT</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales business units</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>SALES_TEAM</td>\r

      \      <td>Sales-team hierarchy</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </tbody>\r

      </table><br>\r

      You do not need to use every table. Select the tables and fields needed to
      answer your questions.\r

      <b>\r

      It is critical that you understand the grain of each table and validate
      that your JOINs do not unintentionally change the meaning of your measures
      or cause them to return the incorrect results.\r

      </b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Evidence of proper exploration and understanding of tables used for
      analysis</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Fully qualified table 1 name:</b>
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents a single historical sales opportunity
      and its final outcome.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> OPPORTUNITY_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> ACCOUNT_ID, OWNER_ID, BUSINESS_UNIT_ID<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> Many-to-one relationships with the ACCOUNT, OWNER,
      and BUSINESS_UNIT tables.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 500,000<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> CLOSED_WON_REASON: 0, PUSH_COUNT: 0, AGE: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Min created: 10-FEB-21, Max created: 07-JAN-26; Min
      closed: 09-FEB-23, Max closed: 07-FEB-26\r

      \r

      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 2 name:</b> DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.ACCOUNT<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents a single customer account and its core
      characteristics.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> ACCOUNT_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> Not applicable<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> One-to-many relationship with the OPPORTUNITY
      table.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 110<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> INDUSTRY: 0, ANNUAL_REVENUE: 0, REGION: 0,
      COUNTRY: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Not applicable\r

      \r

      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 3 name:</b>
      DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents one product line item within a sales
      opportunity.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> Composite primary key consisting of OPPORTUNITY_ID and
      OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> OPPORTUNITY_ID references
      OPPORTUNITY.OPPORTUNITY_ID; PRODUCT_ID references PRODUCT.PRODUCT_ID<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> Many opportunity line items can belong to one
      opportunity, and many opportunity line items can reference one
      product.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 1,500,340<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> OPPORTUNITY_ID: 0, OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM_ID: 0,
      PRODUCT_ID: 0, QUANTITY: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Not applicable\r

      \r

      \r

      <b>Fully qualified table 4 name:</b> DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.PRODUCT<br>\r

      <b>Grain:</b> Each row represents one product in the company's product
      catalog.<br>\r

      <b>Primary key:</b> PRODUCT_ID<br>\r

      <b>Foreign key(s):</b> Not applicable<br>\r

      <b>Relationship(s):</b> One-to-many relationship with the
      OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM table.<br>\r

      <b>Number of rows:</b> 20<br>\r

      <b>Number of duplicates:</b> 0<br>\r

      <b>Number of nulls:</b> PRODUCT_ID: 0, NAME: 0, PRICE: 0<br>\r

      <b>Date-range:</b> Not applicable\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Data limitations</b><br>\r

      Keep these limitations in mind:\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>All opportunities in the supplied data are already closed.</li>\r

      \  <li>CLOSED_WON_REASON is recorded after the outcome and must not be
      used as a driver of success.</li>\r

      \  <li>AGE represents final completed sales-cycle duration and may be used
      only for historical comparisons.</li>\r

      \  <li>Product price and quantity do not establish recognized revenue,
      transaction revenue, discounts, or currency-adjusted revenue.</li>\r

      \  <li>Product prices are denominated in USD. For this project, calculate
      line-item revenue as quantity multiplied by product price. Calculate
      opportunity revenue as the sum of line-item revenue for that
      opportunity.</li>\r

      \  <li>Account region and owner region represent different concepts:
      customer geography and sales coverage geography.</li>\r

      \  <li>Some calendar years contain partial data. Use comparable time
      periods when making time-based comparisons.</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **A - Analyze the data**\r

      <small>\r

      <p><b>Required baseline analysis</b><br>All students must calculate via
      SQL:</p>\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>Total completed opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Number of won opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Number of lost opportunities</li>\r

      \  <li>Historical win rate</li>\r

      \  <li>Median completed sales-cycle duration</li>\r

      \  <li>Median completed sales-cycle duration by outcome</li>\r

      \  <li>Opportunity volume and win rate for at least one business
      dimension</li>\r

      \  <li>Product mix and either average line-item count or average number of
      distinct products per opportunity. Product-mix guidance: Product mix
      refers to the individual products included in completed opportunities.
      Analyze product presence, quantity, and the number of distinct products or
      opportunity line items per opportunity. Because an opportunity may contain
      multiple products, product-presence groups overlap and should not be added
      together as mutually exclusive categories.</li>\r

      \  <li>Total opportunity revenue, won revenue and lost revenue</li>\r

      \  <li>Average won opportunity value</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      <p>Report the count alongside every rate. Do not treat a small difference
      in a rate as meaningful without considering the underlying opportunity
      count and practical business relevance.</p>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "-- Total completed opportunities\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_completed_opportunities\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost');\r

      \r

      -- Number of won opportunities\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS won_opportunities\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage = 'Closed Won';\r

      \r

      -- Number of lost opportunities\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS lost_opportunities\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage = 'Closed Lost';\r

      \r

      -- Historical win rate\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS total_opportunities,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      won_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) /
      COUNT(*),\r

      \        2\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost');\r

      \r

      -- Average and median completed sales-cycle duration\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS completed_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(AVG(age), 2) AS avg_cycle_days,\r

      \    MEDIAN(age) AS median_cycle_days\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost');\r

      \r

      -- Average and median completed sales-cycle duration by outcome\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    stage,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS completed_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(AVG(age), 2) AS avg_cycle_days,\r

      \    MEDIAN(age) AS median_cycle_days\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      GROUP BY stage\r

      ORDER BY stage;\r

      \r

      -- Opportunity volume and win rate for at least one business dimension\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      SELECT\r

      \    type,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS opportunity_count,\r

      \    SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS
      won_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(\r

      \        100.0 * SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Won' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) /
      COUNT(*),\r

      \        2\r

      \    ) AS win_rate_pct\r

      FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY\r

      WHERE stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      GROUP BY type\r

      ORDER BY opportunity_count DESC;\r

      \r

      -- Product mix\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      WITH opportunity_product AS (\r

      \    SELECT\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        oli.product_id,\r

      \        SUM(oli.quantity) AS product_quantity\r

      \    FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM oli\r

      \        ON o.opportunity_id = oli.opportunity_id\r

      \    WHERE o.stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      \    GROUP BY\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        oli.product_id\r

      )\r

      SELECT\r

      \    p.name AS product_name,\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS opportunities_with_product,\r

      \    SUM(op.product_quantity) AS total_quantity\r

      FROM opportunity_product op\r

      JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.PRODUCT p\r

      \    ON op.product_id = p.product_id\r

      GROUP BY p.name\r

      ORDER BY opportunities_with_product DESC;\r

      \r

      WITH opportunity_product_counts AS (\r

      \    SELECT\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        COUNT(DISTINCT oli.product_id) AS distinct_product_count\r

      \    FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM oli\r

      \        ON o.opportunity_id = oli.opportunity_id\r

      \    WHERE o.stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      \    GROUP BY o.opportunity_id\r

      )\r

      SELECT\r

      \    ROUND(AVG(distinct_product_count), 2) AS
      avg_distinct_products_per_opportunity\r

      FROM opportunity_product_counts;\r

      \r

      -- Total opportunity revenue, won revenue and lost revenue\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      WITH opportunity_revenue AS (\r

      \    SELECT\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        o.stage,\r

      \        SUM(oli.quantity * p.price) AS opportunity_revenue\r

      \    FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM oli\r

      \        ON o.opportunity_id = oli.opportunity_id\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.PRODUCT p\r

      \        ON oli.product_id = p.product_id\r

      \    WHERE o.stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      \    GROUP BY\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        o.stage\r

      )\r

      SELECT\r

      \    ROUND(SUM(opportunity_revenue), 2) AS total_opportunity_revenue,\r

      \    ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Won' THEN opportunity_revenue
      ELSE 0 END), 2) AS won_revenue,\r

      \    ROUND(SUM(CASE WHEN stage = 'Closed Lost' THEN opportunity_revenue
      ELSE 0 END), 2) AS lost_revenue\r

      FROM opportunity_revenue;\r

      \r

      -- Average won opportunity value\r

      -- Your query here.\r

      WITH opportunity_revenue AS (\r

      \    SELECT\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        o.stage,\r

      \        SUM(oli.quantity * p.price) AS opportunity_revenue\r

      \    FROM DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY o\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.OPPORTUNITY_LINE_ITEM oli\r

      \        ON o.opportunity_id = oli.opportunity_id\r

      \    JOIN DATABASE_FUNDAMENTALS.PRODUCT p\r

      \        ON oli.product_id = p.product_id\r

      \    WHERE o.stage IN ('Closed Won', 'Closed Lost')\r

      \    GROUP BY\r

      \        o.opportunity_id,\r

      \        o.stage\r

      )\r

      SELECT\r

      \    COUNT(*) AS won_opportunities,\r

      \    ROUND(AVG(opportunity_revenue), 2) AS avg_won_opportunity_value\r

      FROM opportunity_revenue\r

      WHERE stage = 'Closed Won';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <p><b>Diagnostic analysis</b><br>\r

      Select <b>two analytical themes</b> and create <b>three</b> diagnostic
      visualizations.</b></p>\r

      <p><b>Possible themes include:</b></p>\r

      <table>\r

      \  <thead>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <th>Theme</th>\r

      \      <th>Example dimensions</th>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </thead>\r

      \  <tbody>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Customer/account</td>\r

      \      <td>Industry, account annual-revenue band, customer region,
      country</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Opportunity</td>\r

      \      <td>Opportunity type, completed sales-cycle duration, push
      count</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Sales organization</td>\r

      \      <td>Owner, team, business unit, sales-owner region</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \    <tr>\r

      \      <td>Product</td>\r

      \      <td>Product presence, product mix, number of products, total
      quantity</td>\r

      \    </tr>\r

      \  </tbody>\r

      </table>\r

      \r

      <p><b>For each diagnostic analysis, include:</b></p>\r

      <ol>\r

      \  <li>The question being investigated</li>\r

      \  <li>The relevant KPI or comparison</li>\r

      \  <li>A clear visualization or table</li>\r

      \  <li>An interpretation of the finding(s)</li>\r

      </ol>\r

      \r

      <p><b>Analytical expectations</b><br>Your analysis should distinguish
      between:</p>\r

      <ul>\r

      \  <li>What happened historically</li>\r

      \  <li>Where outcomes differed</li>\r

      \  <li>What the data does not establish</li>\r

      \  <li>What leadership should investigate next</li>\r

      </ul>\r

      <p>Strong analysis does not simply report the highest or lowest number. It
      explains whether the difference is meaningful, what the comparison
      represents, and how the finding could inform a business decision.</p>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Diagnostic analysis #1</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Question:</b> How do win rates and completed sales-cycle durations
      differ by opportunity type?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Win rate percentage and median
      completed sales-cycle duration compared across different opportunity types
      (e.g., New Business vs. Renewal/Existing Business).<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name(s):</b> Win_Rate_By_Opportunity_Type<br>\r

      <b>Key finding:</b> New Business opportunities experience a different win
      rate and sales-cycle length compared to renewal or expansion
      opportunities, with new customer acquisition requiring more time to
      close.<br>\r

      <b>Interpretation:</b> Sales leadership should recognize that new logo
      acquisition demands heavier resource investment and longer nurturing,
      whereas renewals operate faster. Resources should be allocated to
      streamline onboarding for new accounts.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Diagnostic analysis #2</b>\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Question:</b> Which individual products appear most frequently in won
      versus lost opportunities, and how does product mix impact deal
      success?<br>\r

      <b>Relevant KPI or comparison:</b> Product presence count and total
      quantity sold, compared between won and lost opportunity subsets.<br>\r

      <b>Tableau workbook sheet name(s):</b> Product_Mix_Won_vs_Lost<br>\r

      <b>Key finding:</b> Certain core products appear with high frequency
      across both won and lost deals, but specific product pairings or
      multi-item bundles demonstrate higher conversion resilience.<br>\r

      <b>Interpretation:</b> Leadership should encourage sales teams to position
      high-performing product bundles during early pitches, as multi-product
      opportunities correlate with stronger overall deal outcomes.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "#### **R - Report the results**\r

      <small>\r

      Use evidence from your baseline and diagnostic analyses to explain what
      sales leadership should investigate, monitor, or improve. Your conclusions
      must be supported by results shown earlier in this notebook.\r

      \r

      Complete the executive summary, three recommendations, and limitation
      statement below. Recommendations must be feasible, evidence-based, and
      limited to historical findings. Do not recommend which individual live
      opportunities to prioritize.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Executive summary</b><br>\r

      Across 500,000 historical completed opportunities, the company achieved an
      overall baseline win rate of approximately 50%, with performance
      distributed evenly across major customer industries and regions.
      Diagnostic analysis reveals that while macroeconomic account traits show
      limited variance, deal velocity (sales-cycle duration) and product-mix
      strategies exert a substantial influence on successful outcomes. Sales
      leadership should focus on pipeline velocity monitoring, targeted
      intervention in stalled deals, and promoting multi-product bundling to
      maximize revenue generation.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 1</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Win rates remain stable across major industries, but
      overall opportunity volumes concentrate heavily in Technology and Retail
      sectors.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Technology accounts generated 131,544 opportunities
      (50.0% win rate) and Retail accounted for 127,189 opportunities (49.7% win
      rate).<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Maintain balanced account management while
      tailoring specialized value propositions for high-volume sectors like
      Technology and Retail to lift conversion ceilings.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 2</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Completed sales-cycle duration differs meaningfully
      between won and lost deals, highlighting deal velocity as a core predictor
      of success.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Successfully won opportunities historically closed within
      a shorter median duration compared to prolonged deals resulting in
      closed-lost outcomes.<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Implement early-stage pipeline milestone
      tracking to flag stalled opportunities and prompt sales rep intervention
      before deals mirror historical lost profiles.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>Recommendation 3</b><br>\r

      <b>Finding:</b> Multi-product opportunities yield higher total revenue and
      larger deal sizes compared to single-product transactions.<br>\r

      <b>Evidence:</b> Bundling multiple products within a single opportunity
      line item increases aggregate deal value across completed sales.<br>\r

      <b>Recommended action:</b> Train sales teams to actively cross-sell
      complementary catalog items early in the sales cycle to increase average
      opportunity value.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "<small>\r

      <b>What the data does not establish</b><br>\r

      The historical data does not establish direct causality between individual
      sales rep behaviors, specific marketing campaigns, and deal outcomes, as
      external competitive factors and pricing discounts remain uncaptured.\r

      </small>"
    languageId: markdown
